{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014hsTRHHxCHaSMemNA9M3Gy","type":"tool_result","content":"219:## Git and commits\n220-\n221-One branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\n222-whatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n223-`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\n224-the process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n225-`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\n226-directly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n227-\n228-## What is written by hand, and what is observed\n229-\n230-By hand: a product decision in `specs/adr/`, the order of the work in `specs/plan.yaml`, where\n231-the owner marks `prete: oui` the epics that run without them, and, for every epic added to the plan,\n232-its dossier in `specs/epics/`, which the plan entry cites as `source:` and the session that writes\n233-its stories reads. The story and the registry entry are\n234-written by hand when the owner drives the story; in a run without them, a session writes them under\n235-the rules of this file. Everything else is observed and recorded by the tool at the moment it\n236-observes it: a red and its message, a Preflight and its revision, a review round and its findings,\n237-the acceptance run and the owner's agreement, the landing. Evidence is written once, in the record of\n238-the step that produced it; elsewhere it is cited. A copy drifts from the code it describes.\n239-\n240-## The cycle without the owner\n241-\n242-`npm run cycle -- suite` runs, one after the other, the epics that `specs/plan.yaml` marks\n243-`prete: oui`, in the order of the plan. For each: the next story of the plan is written by a session\n244-when none is listed to do, it is driven to landing as above, the plan marks it landed, and the epic\n245-becomes `versé` when a session finds that its stories deliver its purpose. The run starts from `main`\n246-with a clean tree and stops at the first block, saying why; it never pushes.\n247-`npm run cycle -- <story> auto` does the same for a single story.\n248-\n249-Three of the owner's answers are delegated, each an act recorded in the record:\n250-\n251-- **Agreement after the acceptance run.** A fresh session, which drove neither the acceptance run\n252-  nor the review, reads the story, the report of the acceptance run and the defects the branch\n253-  records in the registry, then decides `accepte` or `ecart` (`prompts/arbitrage.md`). For each\n254-  defect the branch records, it retains a severity and says why: the registry carries it, the record\n255-  keeps it beside the severity the branch wrote, and the tool stops, naming the defect, when one has\n256-  none. The tool reads the registry with a YAML reader, so it sees an entry whatever valid YAML form\n257-  it is written in, and stops, naming the registry, when the reader does not read it or the branch\n258-  adds an item that is not a defect with an identifier. The owner, when the story waits for them,\n259-  sees those defects with their severity in the question. A gap is a\n3f2bc4c4 docs: the registry records that a preparation copy which cannot be deleted is said under the path it had, not the one it was moved aside to\n701290f8 docs: the registry records fixed the test of a detached process that could not tell the survey's Seatbelt rule from one giving the surveyed directory itself\na19079ef test: after the survey of a control, a process it detached does not make again the path under which its copy was surveyed, aiming at it by that path\nff91a0d3 docs: the registry records fixed the survey's unremovable paths, removed, the run-scoped rule alone keeping a surveyed run from removing anything in its copy\ne115f907 refactor: a surveyed run under Seatbelt is kept from removing anything in its copy by its run-scoped rule alone, the unremovable paths that repeated it being gone from the sandbox profile\ne1164e8f fix: under macOS, a copy is deleted whatever user flags or ACLs the code run in it left: before removing it, every entry its walk finds without following a link has its uchg and uappnd flags cleared, its ACL removed and its mode opened to its owner, pass after pass until nothing is left closed\n067e57d9 test: under macOS, a copy where the code left a closed directory carrying an ACL that denies deletion and a file flagged uchg is deleted, and nothing a link of the copy leads to is changed\n9105b116 docs: the registry records that the survey's unremovable paths add nothing under Seatbelt, whose run-scoped rule already refuses removal, and that no other backend reads them\ncf8fd3b5 test: granting a control a directory refuses a config.json that cannot be read and leaves it as it was, and writes one giving the directory when the data directory holds none\n91066b2c fix: a surveyed run under Seatbelt may only create entries and write files in its copy, even under a temporary path, so no mode, flag, owner, extended attribute or ACL it sets keeps the copy from being deleted\n924f5e22 test: under Seatbelt, a surveyed control may only create and write files in its copy: changing a file's modes, flags, owner, extended attributes or ACLs is refused, even under a temporary path, and the copy can be deleted afterwards\nff7255c5 docs: the registry records that no test holds the survey's Seatbelt rule against remaking the surveyed path, and that no macOS run exercises its bubblewrap arguments\n7303c125 docs: the registry records fixed the survey's walk without links, now held by a test whose link leads where the control may write\n8d9a8830 test: the survey of a control lists its copy without following its links, so a file written through a link to a directory the control may write is not returned\nddf11b2a test: the kernel tests of the offered 